Here’s the refined, owner-friendly format:

---
### 📄 Log Format (Text File)
```
[01.05.24  12:38:33] Aguga2201
Dimension: Overworld | Coords: (128, 64, -52)
Items Added: [192 gold_ingot, 6 oak_log]
Items Removed: [26 diamond, 2 netherite_ingot]
---
```

### 🔍 Why This Works Better for Admin Oversight
| Element | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `[DD.MM.YY  HH:MM:SS]` | Keeps your exact timestamp style. Sortable in text editors. |
| `Player Name` | Instantly identifies who made changes. |
| `Dimension | Coords` | Critical for tracking where items are moving. No guessing. |
| `Items Added/Removed` | Only logs actual net changes. Ignores empty opens/closes. |
| `---` separator | Makes log files easy to split, grep, or parse later if needed. |

---
### 📁 File Structure Options
You mentioned the old mod used **one file per chest type**. I can implement either:

| Option | Pros | Cons |
|--------|------|------|
| **Single log**: `config/admin-tools/chest_interactions.log` | Easier to search, no file bloat, simpler writer | Slightly harder to "focus" on one block type |
| **Per-block files**: `chest.log`, `barrel.log`, `shulker.log`, etc. | Matches your old setup, easy to isolate | 10+ files can get messy, harder to cross-reference |

**My recommendation:** Single log. It’s cleaner, faster to write, and you can still filter by block type in any text editor (`Ctrl+F "minecraft:barrel"`). But I’ll code it so you can toggle per-file mode in config if you prefer.

---
### ⚙️ Implementation Rules (Agent-Ready)
```
- Format: Strictly text. No CSV/JSON. UTF-8 encoding.
- Line structure: Exact match to format above. Never modify layout.
- Writer: Single-threaded async queue. Flushes to disk every 30s or on server shutdown.
- Size management: Auto-rotate at 50MB (configurable). Oldest entries drop off.
- Metadata: Raw NBT stripped. Only item_id + count logged.
- Location: Dimension string + chunk coordinates. Never null.
- Access: Zero in-game commands. Zero permissions. File lives in config folder. Owner-only via FTP/Explorer.
- Performance: Event-driven. Only writes when items are actually added/removed. No tick loops.
```

---
